the star wars film series is one of the  most commercially and critically  successful franchises in history ticket  sales alone of yielded over 9.5 billion  dollars worldwide to date and that's not  counting merch sales licensing deals and  other profits since its start in 1977  the series has grown from a trilogy of  well-received fantasy movies into a  full-blown worldwide institution plotted  for its ability to excite and inspire  audiences of course this isn't to say  that the Star Wars franchise is  completely perfect far from it fans can  and will pick apart the movies and find  dozens of slip-ups and make a laundry  list of less than awesome moments that  slightly mar the overall pictures but  those little moments would be small  potatoes in comparison to the mistake  Disney has admitted to making with the  Star Wars franchise one that the studio  is trying to remedy as we speak during a  September 2018 interview with The  Hollywood Reporter Walt Disney Company  CEO Bob Iger addressed the fact that  after acquiring Lucasfilm for just over  four billion dollars in 2012 Disney has  churned out one Star Wars movie every  year since 2015 specifically those  movies would be JJ Abrams as Episode  seven the force awakens the series first  anthology film rogue one in 2016 the  Rian Johnson directed Episode eight the  last Jedi in December 2017 and the young  Han Solo movie simply called solo just  five months later in May 2018 when  reporter Matthew Bologna brought up the  seemingly growing impression that Disney  should pump the brakes on it's Star Wars  releases and not put out a new movie  each year  eager surprisingly agreed he actually  admitted that the huge volume of  releases right out of the gate was a  total mistake and even took personal  responsibility for the evident fatigue  inducing misstep Uyghur confessed as I  look back I think the mistake that I  made I take the blame was a little too  much too fast looking ahead the CEO said  that Disney will no longer be producing  Star Wars movies at such an accelerated  pace according to Uyghur fans can expect  some slowdown following the release of  JJ Abrams as episode 9 although no one  should expect the house of mouse to  bring things to a total halt as Eiger  explained the state of Star Wars movies  beyond 20 19 episode 9 will still be  robust just with a little more breathing  room he reminded readers that Game of  Thrones directors David  and DB Weiss are actively developing  their own Star Wars saga about which  very little is currently known last we  heard Rian Johnson was still tinkering  away with development on his own film  trilogy the company is also developing  at least one big-budget TV show for its  upcoming streaming service but this  content Uyghur said will be served up a  little more judiciously but the company  being quote a little more careful about  volume and timing the suggestion that  Disney should throttle down on the Star  Wars movies isn't a particularly new  idea fans and commentators have been  talking about the potential consequences  of Star Wars over saturation for a while  with the once unthinkable term Star Wars  fatigue becoming a frequent talking  point the franchise took a major hit  with the massively underperforming solo  a Star Wars story which essentially  bombed at the box office after a  notoriously troubled production  following that high-profile  disappointment a whole lot of people got  on board with the idea that Disney  strategy for Star Wars was actually  killing interest in the once venerable  franchise the common wisdom was that  Lucasfilm was trying too hard to mimic  the release calendar of Marvel Studios  and it's backfiring in a big way while  there are fans and viewers who are  perfectly happy with the current state  of Star Wars there's a vocal segment of  the fan base that's all but begging for  Disney to get its act together as the  series wraps up the Skywalker saga and  moves on to stories beyond  iger's comments seem to suggest that  those fans are finally being heard  though it's refreshing to hear I grrrrrr  straight up admitting Disney dropped the  ball  is it possible that the damage is  already done that's hard to predict  already some feel that Disney has bigger  problems with its Star Wars approach  than the high volume of releases after  the interview thr writer Graham  Macmillan questioned whether volume and  timing are really to blame and suggested  that perhaps the audience has a problem  with these specific Star Wars movies in  other words a lot of people might just  dislike the stories that Disney is  telling and that's a harder problem to  fix than just stretching out a release  schedule still it seems that the people  in charge at Disney are doing some  serious reflection about how they might  have mangled they're very expensive  space fantasy series a less dense  release schedule is a good start as to  whether or not that will be enough to  bring the magic back to Star Wars that  remains to be seen  [Music]
 

